Title:
windows_kernel_shadow_stack (Windows 11 kernel shadow stack PoCs)

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This repository contains a Windows kernel driver + user-mode client that demonstrates behaviors and failure modes of Microsoft’s kernel shadow stack mitigation (CET Shadow Stack) on Windows 11 24H2. It provides PoCs that intentionally tamper with kernel return addresses, stack frames, CET enablement, MSRs, and shadow stack memory/PTEs to observe enforcement—typically resulting in controlled BSODs. The tool uses IOCTLs for user-to-kernel communication, letting the client request specific kernel-level actions. It also includes a division-by-zero PoC to illustrate interactions between exception handling (`try/except`) and shadow stack behavior. It’s mainly useful for Windows kernel security researchers, exploit developers, and red teamers studying modern control-flow integrity and its impact on kernel exploitation. It’s interesting because it offers hands-on, reproducible experiments around CET/VBS/HVCI-protected kernel control-flow, specifically tuned for 24H2 offsets.

Technical Focus:
- Windows 11 24H2 kernel CET shadow stacks (KernelShadowStacks)
- Kernel-mode stack/return-address manipulation and frame skipping
- CR4 CET toggling and CPU control register behavior
- MSR writes and resulting kernel stability/enforcement
- Shadow stack memory writes and shadow stack PTE tampering
- IOCTL-based user-mode ↔ kernel-mode driver control plane

Use Cases:
- Reproduce and study BSOD conditions caused by shadow stack / CET violations
- Validate how kernel shadow stacks react to return-address and stack-frame tampering
- Explore how VBS/HVCI + KernelShadowStacks change kernel exploitation assumptions
- Build a lab for testing kernel exception handling interactions (e.g., divide-by-zero) under CET
- Develop/verify kernel offset handling and version-specific mitigation research (24H2)

Keywords:
Windows 11 24H2, kernel shadow stack, CET, Control-flow Enforcement Technology, KernelShadowStacks, VBS, HVCI, Device Guard, CR4, MSR, wrmsr, PTE, page tables, IOCTL, Windows kernel driver, return address, stack frame, BSOD, try/except, WDK, SDK